# Ignore Python compiled files
*.pyc
__pycache__/
.env
# No trailing slash on purpose: `.venv/` matches only a directory, so a `.venv`
# SYMLINK into a shared venv (how a throwaway worktree borrows one) slipped
# past it and was committed by a `git add -A`. The slash-less spelling matches
# a file, a symlink and a directory alike, so it is the one that covers this.
.venv
.DS_Store
dist/
node_modules/
# pnpm's content-addressable store. Normally it lives once under ~/Library or
# ~/.local/share and never appears here — but pnpm hard-links from the store
# into node_modules, and hard links cannot cross filesystems, so a checkout on
# a DIFFERENT filesystem than the home store (a `git worktree add /tmp/...`,
# which is how this repo is routinely worked) makes pnpm fall back to a local
# store beside the package. That is 34 MB of regenerable cache that a
# `git add -A` in such a worktree would otherwise commit.
.pnpm-store/
local-operator-extension.zip
# google-github-actions/auth creates these only when credential-file export is
# enabled; ignore them defensively so a local workflow experiment cannot commit
# a short-lived credential alongside the extension artifact.
gha-creds-*.json
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.vscode/
.coverage*
htmlcov/
build/
# pytest-textual-snapshot report artifact — regenerable, and it embeds the
# environment (including secrets) in its HTML dump. Never commit it.
snapshot_report.html

# `uv` resolution artifact. This project pins nothing through it: CI and the
# documented install both run `pip install -e ".[dev]"` against pyproject, so a
# committed lockfile is a second source of truth that nothing reads and no job
# validates. It landed by accident in a TUI commit.
#
# EXCEPTION: an adapter distribution under `benchmarks/` IS a separately-locked
# workspace — its `uv.lock` is the pinned dependency set the wheel and digests
# are attested against, so those locks are committed deliberately.
uv.lock
!benchmarks/*/uv.lock

# Throwaway validation aids: the tidy-tree prototype and the captured org-chart
# screenshot matrix live here so the reviewer/designer can look at them, but
# they are not shipped code — committing them would put the prototype (which is
# deliberately not type-clean) under the pyright/black gate and re-bill the
# SVGs as repo weight. The shipped code is the promoted `org_layout.py`.
scratch/
